Day 40 /100 of #DrGVishwanathanChallenge
Solved two interesting Greedy problems on LeetCode today!
Problem 1. Leetcode(45 Jump Game II):
Approach:
 Used a Greedy + Range Expansion approach. While traversing the array, I kept track of the farthest index that can be reached within the current jump range. Whenever I reached the end of the current range, I incremented the jump count and updated the range to the farthest reachable index. This guarantees the minimum number of jumps required.

Time Complexity: O(n)
 Space Complexity: O(1)

Problem 2. Leetcode:( 678. Valid Parenthesis String) :
Approach:
 Used a Greedy Range technique by maintaining two counters:
low → minimum possible number of unmatched '('
high → maximum possible number of unmatched '('
For every '*', it can behave as '(', ')', or an empty character, so the range is updated accordingly. If high becomes negative, the string is invalid. At the end, if low == 0, a valid interpretation exists.

Time Complexity: O(n)
 Space Complexity: O(1)

-> Key Takeaways
1) Learned how greedy algorithms can make optimal decisions using local information.
2) Understood the concept of maintaining boundaries/ranges instead of checking every possible case.
3) Strengthened my ability to identify when a greedy solution is more efficient than simulation or dynamic programming.
